          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "\\frac{16}{64}=\\frac{1\\cancel{6}}{\\cancel{6}4}=\\frac14",
              "display": true
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p18-q21-task",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Find all positive fractions with this property, which are less than 1 in value, and which have two digits in both their numerator and denominator, none of which are zero.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\frac{16}{64},\\ \\frac{19}{95},\\ \\frac{26}{65},\\ \\frac{49}{98}$.\n\nCheck deletion of each common digit against the original fraction.",
      "worked": "The four fractions are\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{16}{64}&=\\frac14\\\\\\frac{19}{95}&=\\frac15\\\\\\frac{26}{65}&=\\frac25\\\\\\frac{49}{98}&=\\frac48=\\frac12.\\end{align*}$$\nDeleting the common digit gives respectively $\\frac14,\\frac15,\\frac25,\\frac48$.\n\nFor completeness, let the remaining numerator and denominator digits be $a,b$, with $1\\le a<b\\le9$, and let the deleted digit be $c$, with $1\\le c\\le9$. Deleting two tens digits from $\\frac{10c+a}{10c+b}$, or two units digits from $\\frac{10a+c}{10b+c}$, preserves the value only when $a=b$, which would give 1. For opposite positions, $\\frac{10c+a}{10b+c}=\\frac ab$ would require $c(10b-a)=9ab$. Thus $c=\\frac{9ab}{10b-a}$. Since $a<b$ and $a\\le8$, we have $a-1<\\frac{9a}{10}<c<a$. There is no integer strictly between $a-1$ and $a$, so this arrangement cannot qualify.\n\nThe remaining arrangement is $\\frac{10a+c}{10c+b}=\\frac ab$, giving\n$$c(10a-b)=9ab.$$\nFor each digit $a=1,\\ldots,8$ and $b=a+1,\\ldots,9$, retain only values $c=\\frac{9ab}{10a-b}$ that are integers from 1 to 9. These give exactly $(a,b,c)=(1,4,6),(1,5,9),(2,5,6),(4,8,9)$, and hence exactly the four fractions listed.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 154
  },
